The Mahalonobis distance (Mahal) is defined as the vector distance measured between
a point and the center of a Gaussian distribution; it is computed using the inverse covariance of that distribution as a metric. See Figure 3-5. Intuitively, this is analogous
to the z-score in basic statistics, where the distance from the center of a distribution is
measured in units of the variance of that distribution. The Mahalonobis distance is just
a multivariable generalization of the same idea.
cvMahalonobis() computes the value:

rMahalonobis = ( x − μ )T Σ−1 ( x −μ )

The vector vec1 is presumed to be the point x, and the vector vec2 is taken to be the distribution’s mean.* That matrix mat is the inverse covariance.
In practice, this covariance matrix will usually have been computed with cvCalcCovar
Matrix() (described previously) and then inverted with cvInvert(). It is good programming practice to use the SV_SVD method for this inversion because someday you will encounter a distribution for which one of the eigenvalues is 0!

